2026-03-02 01:45:01,252 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 01:45:04,860 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hi. I'm feeling pretty anxious about this test that's coming up in college. It's a major test and I believe my future depends on me doing well on it.
2026-03-02 01:45:05,361 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds really important to you. Can you tell me more about what specifically makes you feel anxious about the test? Is it the content, the pressure to perform, or something else?
2026-03-02 01:45:08,196 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think it's just the pressure of knowing that I put in all this effort and studying but that it could all be for nothing.
2026-03-02 01:45:08,776 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s understandable to feel that way when you’ve invested so much effort. What happens when you think about that possibility—what if everything you studied doesn’t pay off? Can you describe that thought in more detail?
2026-03-02 01:45:13,610 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I just freeze up and then I'm not able to remember everything that I studied. I've studied a lot and practiced questions, but I'm afraid it won't be enough.
2026-03-02 01:45:14,196 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s easy to feel overwhelmed when you believe your performance depends entirely on memory. What if instead of focusing on remembering everything, you shifted your focus to doing your best in the moment—what would that feel like?
2026-03-02 01:45:19,716 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that would help a lot because I have studied so much and practiced. I know I can do my best, but it's the fear of not knowing what to do in that moment that is so anxiety-inducing
2026-03-02 01:45:20,719 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful insight. You’re not failing because you don’t know the material—you’re failing because you’re afraid of not being able to access it in the moment. What if you tried to reframe that fear into a simple, calm intention before the test, like “I’ll do my best, and I’ll trust what I’ve learned”? How does that sound?
2026-03-02 01:45:23,146 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds really helpful actually. I think I'll try that before the test.
2026-03-02 01:45:23,827 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great choice. You’re already taking a strong step by shifting from fear to trust. How would you like to practice that intention before the test—maybe in your mind, or out loud? I’m here if you’d like to go deeper.
2026-03-02 01:45:28,402 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I can say it out loud before the test. I'll say "I trust that I have learned this material and I will do my best."
2026-03-02 01:45:28,952 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a clear, powerful statement. Saying it out loud builds confidence and grounds you in your capabilities. How would you feel if you said it just before you walk into the test room?
2026-03-02 01:45:33,146 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll feel a lot calmer and more confident going into the test. I think I'll also be able to breathe better.
2026-03-02 01:45:33,808 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful outcome. Calm breathing and confidence are signs your mind is starting to work with you, not against you. Would you like to practice that statement again—just in your mind—before you go to sleep tonight?
2026-03-02 01:45:36,606 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, I can try that. Thank you. I think that will really help.
2026-03-02 01:45:37,146 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. Remember, you're not alone in this. If you ever want to talk more about it, or need to practice again, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 01:45:38,244 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. Goodnight.
2026-03-02 01:45:38,277 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 01:45:38,598 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're welcome. Sleep well, and carry that calm intention with you. You’ve got this. 😊
2026-03-02 01:45:40,959 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. I'll try to remember to breathe more often too.
2026-03-02 01:45:40,992 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 01:45:41,501 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a wonderful practice—breathing helps ground you in the present. You’re already building strong habits. I’ll see you when you’re ready to talk more. Goodnight.
