2026-03-02 01:49:47,121 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 01:49:52,625 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I've been feeling a bit lonely lately. I've been doing well at school, I'm keeping up with my courses and I'm doing quite well in them, but I just feel really exhausted all the time.
2026-03-02 01:49:53,178 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds tough—feeling lonely and exhausted even when you're doing well academically. What does the loneliness feel like? Is it a constant presence, or does it come up in certain situations?
2026-03-02 01:49:59,500 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I guess it comes up when I get home from school. My partner is in jail right now, so he's not here. My mom moved to another state when I was 18, so I don't really talk to her.
2026-03-02 01:50:00,076 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. Losing consistent connection with your partner and mom must feel isolating, especially when you're already tired. How does that absence affect your energy or mood when you're alone at home?
2026-03-02 01:50:04,111 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel a bit empty. I feel like I'm doing all the work, and I don't really have anybody to talk to.
2026-03-02 01:50:04,805 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It makes sense that you'd feel empty when you're doing all the work without someone to share it with. What would it look like, even small, if you could have one moment—just one—where you feel heard or connected, even briefly?
2026-03-02 01:50:08,008 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I guess I would feel understood. I would feel like I'm not the only one going through this.
2026-03-02 01:50:08,810 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful and important feeling—being understood can make a big difference. What if we started small, like setting a gentle intention to reach out to someone, even just to say, “I’m feeling tired and lonely today—can I talk for a minute?” How does that sound?
2026-03-02 01:50:11,992 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds like a good idea. I think I might feel anxious if I say I'm lonely.
2026-03-02 01:50:12,721 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s completely valid—fear of judgment or being seen as weak can make it hard to share. What if we reframe it as simply saying, “I’m feeling tired and a little lonely today,” without expecting anything in return? How does that feel?
2026-03-02 01:50:16,191 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that would be better. I think it would make me feel more comfortable talking to my friends.
2026-03-02 01:50:16,902 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and brave step. Starting with a simple, honest statement can open the door to connection without pressure. Would you like to try saying it to one friend this week—just once? I’m here if you want to practice it together.
2026-03-02 01:50:19,465 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds like a good idea. Thank you for your help today.
2026-03-02 01:50:20,044 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. Remember, it’s okay to take things one step at a time. If you’d like to check in again or try something new later, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 01:50:21,071 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thanks. You too.
2026-03-02 01:50:21,103 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 01:50:21,524 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're welcome. Take care of yourself, and remember—you're not alone in this. I'll be here if you ever want to talk again.
2026-03-02 01:50:23,082 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. Have a good day.
2026-03-02 01:50:23,115 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 01:50:23,401 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You too. Wishing you a calm and gentle day. Reach out whenever you're ready.
