2026-03-02 01:57:37,010 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 01:57:40,126 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hi! I am feeling a little anxious. I have a big test in one of my college classes and I feel like my future depends on it.
2026-03-02 01:57:40,646 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like the test is carrying a lot of weight for you right now. What does that anxiety feel like in your body—any specific sensations or thoughts?
2026-03-02 01:57:43,304 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel a little bit of a knot in my stomach. I also have trouble sleeping because I am anxious about the test.
2026-03-02 01:57:43,844 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a common physical response to anxiety—your body is alerting you to the stress. Have you noticed any specific thoughts about failing the test or not doing well that come up at night?
2026-03-02 01:57:48,077 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I am afraid that I will freeze up when I take the test. I have studied so hard for this test but I still feel like it won't be enough.
2026-03-02 01:57:48,615 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s understandable to feel that way, especially after putting in so much effort. What evidence do you have that you *can* do well, even if you feel like you might freeze?
2026-03-02 01:57:52,158 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I have practiced problems similar to the ones that will be on the test. I have also made flashcards and studied with my friends.
2026-03-02 01:57:52,797 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s strong evidence—you’ve taken real, consistent steps to prepare. What would it look like if you tried one small thing during the test—like taking a deep breath before starting—to see if it helps you feel more grounded?
2026-03-02 01:57:56,238 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could try that. I think that I will feel more relaxed if I take some deep breaths before I start the test.
2026-03-02 01:57:56,798 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and practical step. How would you like to practice that breathing before the test—maybe just a few minutes before you begin? Would you like to try a simple version together?
2026-03-02 01:57:59,662 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, that sounds good. Thank you. I would like to try that before the test.
2026-03-02 01:58:00,237 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great choice. You're already taking an active step toward feeling more in control. When you try the breathing, how would you like to check in with yourself afterward—just to notice how you felt?
2026-03-02 01:58:05,426 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could try to take a few practice exams to see how I do. If I do well on the practice exams then I will feel more confident going into the actual test.
2026-03-02 01:58:06,003 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a smart and constructive plan. What would it look like to do one practice exam right now—maybe in the next few days—and how would you like to track your results or feelings after?
2026-03-02 01:58:10,237 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could write down my results and see how I do. I will take a practice exam in a few days and see how I do.
2026-03-02 01:58:10,839 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a clear and manageable next step. When you review your practice exam results, what specific part of your performance would you like to notice—like your confidence, time management, or understanding of certain topics?
2026-03-02 01:58:15,700 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I would like to see how well I do on the test. I want to see if the practice exams help me do better on the actual test.
2026-03-02 01:58:16,311 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s natural to want to see progress. What would you do if you noticed you did well on the practice exam—what would that feel like, and how might it change your mindset before the real test?
2026-03-02 01:58:20,092 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I would feel more confident going into the test. I would feel better knowing that the practice exams are helping me.
2026-03-02 01:58:20,829 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful shift in mindset. Confidence built from your own efforts can make a real difference. Would you like to set a small reminder for yourself—like a note or a daily check-in—to keep noticing how you're feeling as the test date approaches?
2026-03-02 01:58:24,427 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could put a note in my phone to remind me to take deep breaths before the test.
2026-03-02 01:58:25,059 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and practical habit to build. How might you say it to yourself when you see the reminder—something kind and grounded, like “I’m ready, I’ve prepared, I can do this”?
2026-03-02 01:58:29,704 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could try that. I would like to say something kind to myself. I could say something like “I’ve got this.”
2026-03-02 01:58:30,376 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: “ healing this” is a strong and reassuring statement. Using it when you feel anxious can help shift your inner tone. Would you like to try saying it out loud once before the test—just to feel it in your body?
2026-03-02 01:58:34,365 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, that sounds good. I think it will help me relax and feel more confident going into the test.
2026-03-02 01:58:35,056 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a great intention. You’re building small, powerful habits that support your confidence and calm. How would you like to check in with yourself after the test—just to notice how you felt, even if it wasn’t perfect?
2026-03-02 01:58:38,408 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I will let you know how I do on the test and how I felt during it.
2026-03-02 01:58:39,192 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and kind way to reflect. Remember, your progress isn’t just about the outcome—it’s about how you’re showing up, preparing, and caring for yourself along the way. I’m here if you’d like to talk more when you’re ready.
2026-03-02 01:58:41,495 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you! I appreciate it. Have a good day!
2026-03-02 01:58:41,532 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 01:58:42,108 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. Take care of yourself, and remember—you’ve already taken important steps toward feeling more confident. I’ll be here if you’d like to talk again. Have a peaceful day.
2026-03-02 01:58:43,818 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: You too! Thank you. Bye.
2026-03-02 01:58:43,851 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 01:58:44,428 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for being open and honest. You're doing well by taking care of your mind and emotions. I’ll see you when you’re ready. Take care, and have a restful day.
