2026-03-02 02:01:22,392 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:01:26,705 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I've been feeling pretty down lately. I'm struggling to see a future for myself and I'm jealous of others around me who have already achieved more than I have.
2026-03-02 02:01:27,201 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like you're carrying a lot of emotional weight right now. Can you tell me more about what specific achievements or situations make you feel jealous?
2026-03-02 02:01:34,690 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think what bothers me the most is when I see others achieve their goals. My cousin, who I used to look after when she was younger because her parents were busy working, recently bought a house and I didn't even get a card or a thank you.
2026-03-02 02:01:35,391 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That must feel unfair and disappointing. It’s natural to feel overlooked when others seem to receive recognition or rewards while you don’t. What would it mean for you if you were to acknowledge your own efforts or value, even if they’re not visible to others?
2026-03-02 02:01:38,714 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I guess it would mean that I'm not worthless and that I'm doing my best with what I have.
2026-03-02 02:01:39,491 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful realization. Recognizing your own value—even when it’s not seen by others—can be a strong foundation for self-worth. How might you start to notice or celebrate one small thing you’ve done recently, even if it didn’t lead to a big outcome?
2026-03-02 02:01:42,559 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I guess I could focus more on what I'm doing right rather than what I'm doing wrong.
2026-03-02 02:01:43,316 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful shift. Focusing on what you’re doing right can build confidence and reduce feelings of comparison. What’s one small action or choice you made recently that you could reflect on as something you did well, even if it didn’t lead to a big result?
2026-03-02 02:01:46,301 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think it would be choosing to study instead of watching TV or going out with friends.
2026-03-02 02:01:47,074 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a great example—a simple but meaningful choice. Studying shows you’re investing in your future, even when it’s not immediately visible. How might you gently reinforce that choice with yourself, maybe by noting it in a journal or saying it out loud when you feel down?
2026-03-02 02:01:50,396 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think it would be helpful to remind myself of my goals and why I'm working towards them.
