2026-03-02 02:06:15,042 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:06:16,752 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm alright, still trying to figure out what's going on with my depression
2026-03-02 02:06:17,467 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That's a thoughtful thing to notice—recognizing that you're still working through it can be a strong first step. What does "trying to figure out" feel like for you? Is there a specific thought or emotion that stands out when you think about your depression?
2026-03-02 02:06:20,721 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I guess I feel like I don't know what I need to get better or what's causing it. I have been feeling very unmotivated lately.
2026-03-02 02:06:21,311 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It's common to feel uncertain about what's needed when depression shows up. Can you think of a recent time when you felt especially unmotivated—what was going on in your mind or body at that moment?
2026-03-02 02:06:26,058 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I guess just sitting in front of my laptop for hours trying to work on research papers. I know I need to do it but I just can't seem to get myself to start.
2026-03-02 02:06:26,745 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds really frustrating—knowing you need to work but struggling to begin. What happens in your mind when you think about starting a research paper? Is there a thought like "I’ll never get it done" or "It’s too hard"?
2026-03-02 02:06:29,617 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think more like "I'm a failure and can't do this" or that I'm not good enough
2026-03-02 02:06:30,371 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Those thoughts—like "I'm a failure" or "I'm not good enough"—can make starting feel impossible. Can you think of a time when you *did* succeed at something small, even if it was just a tiny step? What did that feel like?
2026-03-02 02:06:33,744 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yeah, like when I cook a good meal. It's not hard to cook but it feels good when it turns out well
2026-03-02 02:06:34,640 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful example—cooking a good meal shows you *do* have the ability to succeed, even in small ways. What if we tried a small experiment: next time you feel stuck on a paper, instead of starting, just spend five minutes setting up your workspace or writing one sentence? How would that feel?
2026-03-02 02:06:40,113 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I would feel a lot better than if I just sat there not doing anything. But sometimes I just get so unmotivated that it's hard to even get myself to start
2026-03-02 02:06:40,976 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely valid to feel stuck—unmotivated doesn’t mean you’re failing. What if we tried something gentle: when you notice that feeling coming, pause and ask yourself, “What’s one small thing I could do right now, even if it’s just a tiny step?” How does that sound?
2026-03-02 02:06:44,047 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds great! And if I feel like that's not enough I could ask you for more suggestions
2026-03-02 02:06:44,829 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great—that’s a strong sign of self-awareness. You’re already building a plan with care. When you feel stuck again, just bring that small step to mind: “What’s one tiny thing I can do?” I’m here to help refine it if you’d like.
2026-03-02 02:06:47,030 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you so much! I will try this out for sure.
2026-03-02 02:06:47,718 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. That’s a meaningful step—being open to trying small things is already a form of courage. Let me know how it goes, or if you’d like to explore another small step together. You’ve got this.
2026-03-02 02:06:49,928 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I appreciate your help! I think this will help a lot!
2026-03-02 02:06:49,965 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
