2026-03-02 02:18:50,306 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:18:52,956 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hi. I've been better. I am still recovering from a long week. How are you?
2026-03-02 02:18:53,387 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thanks for sharing that. It sounds like you're being mindful of your recovery—what’s one small thing that helped you feel a bit better this week?
2026-03-02 02:18:56,715 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I made it through one of my hardest classes this week and only felt like I was on the verge of a breakdown once.
2026-03-02 02:18:57,030 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a significant achievement. What small moment or action helped you stay grounded during that moment of strain?
2026-03-02 02:18:59,589 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I took a walk outside during my break. It was nice to get some fresh air.
2026-03-02 02:19:00,015 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful choice—taking a walk can shift your mindset and reset your focus. What did you notice about your thoughts or feelings while walking?
2026-03-02 02:19:04,041 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I was able to put some of my problems in perspective and realize that there are others out there who are suffering more than I am.
2026-03-02 02:19:04,521 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful realization. Seeing others’ struggles can reduce self-blame and bring a sense of connection. Has that perspective helped you feel more compassionate toward yourself lately?
2026-03-02 02:19:06,551 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes. I have been working on being kinder to myself.
2026-03-02 02:19:07,022 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a strong and important step. What’s one small self-compassionate action you’ve tried recently—like speaking to yourself like you would to a friend?
2026-03-02 02:19:09,501 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I've been trying to remind myself that it's okay to not be okay.
2026-03-02 02:19:10,090 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s deeply valid and powerful. Reminding yourself that it’s okay to not be okay creates space for healing. Have you noticed any shift in how you respond when stress comes up since saying that to yourself?
2026-03-02 02:19:13,248 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel less like I need to push through and more like I am able to stop and breathe.
2026-03-02 02:19:13,751 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful shift—giving yourself permission to pause is a form of self-care. How does that sense of stillness feel when you return to your tasks afterward?
2026-03-02 02:19:14,697 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It feels more manageable.
2026-03-02 02:19:15,252 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a great sign. Small changes like pausing to breathe can build resilience over time. Is there one moment this week when you’ll intentionally pause and take a breath when things feel overwhelming?
2026-03-02 02:19:16,517 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I will definitely do that again.
2026-03-02 02:19:17,076 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great—your intention to return to that practice is a strong step forward. Let me know how it goes, or if you’d like to talk more about it later. You're doing well.
2026-03-02 02:19:19,457 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thanks. I will keep you updated. Have a good evening.
2026-03-02 02:19:19,490 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:19:20,018 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're welcome. Remember, you don’t have to go through it all alone. I’m here if you’d like to check in again. Take care, and have a restful evening.
2026-03-02 02:19:21,045 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: You too. Thanks.
2026-03-02 02:19:21,078 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:19:21,449 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing. Take care of yourself, and remember to return when you’re ready. I’m here. 🌿
