2026-03-02 02:21:50,477 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:21:53,460 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I have been better. I have a lot of suicidal thoughts and I'm trying to find purpose in my life.
2026-03-02 02:21:53,976 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. Suicidal thoughts are serious — it’s important you don’t go through this alone. Can you tell me more about what’s triggering those thoughts right now?
2026-03-02 02:21:59,273 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I lost my girlfriend 5 months ago to suicide. We were planning on getting married and having a kid. I feel guilty because I don't think I did everything I could have done to prevent her death
2026-03-02 02:22:00,005 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: I hear how deeply you’re feeling this guilt and loss. It’s natural to wonder what you could have done differently when someone you love is gone. Can you tell me what specific thoughts or moments from the time before her passing made you feel like you didn’t do enough?
2026-03-02 02:22:04,456 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: The last time I saw her she was okay and we said our goodbyes. I should have hugged her and kissed her. Maybe that would have made her okay
2026-03-02 02:22:05,232 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s heartbreaking that you feel you missed that moment — and it’s understandable that it weighs on you. Hugging and kissing might have felt like small things, but they carry deep emotional weight. Would you like to explore how that moment might be shaping your current feelings about guilt and purpose?
2026-03-02 02:22:08,392 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, it's shaped them a lot. It's like I'm just drifting through life with no purpose
2026-03-02 02:22:09,158 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That moment of saying goodbye might feel like a turning point — not because it changed her fate, but because it changed how you see your own role in love and connection. What would it mean to you to find a small, meaningful way to honor her memory in your daily life?
2026-03-02 02:22:12,362 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm not sure. She meant the world to me and it's hard to see life without her.
2026-03-02 02:22:13,302 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely valid to feel lost without her. The love you shared is real and profound — and that love can still guide you, even in quiet, everyday ways. What if we started by noticing one small thing each day that feels like a tribute to her? Something simple, like a shared memory, a song, or a moment of kindness?
2026-03-02 02:22:15,658 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could do that. I will try to think of something each day
2026-03-02 02:22:16,283 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful first step. Even small acts of remembrance can create a sense of continuity and meaning. How does it feel to take that first step today — to notice one small thing that connects you to her?
2026-03-02 02:22:19,126 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I will try and do it tonight. I hope it helps. Thanks for listening.
2026-03-02 02:22:19,814 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for being brave enough to share this. Your willingness to try, even in small ways, is already a form of healing. If you ever want to talk more or just need a moment of support, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:22:24,570 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you so much. This means a lot. I will try to remember some small thing each day and see if it makes a difference
2026-03-02 02:22:24,607 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:22:25,188 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s deeply meaningful. Small memories can become quiet anchors in a hard journey. I’m truly glad I could walk with you through this. If you ever want to reflect again, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:22:28,772 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you so much. I hope you have a great day. I think we're done for today
2026-03-02 02:22:28,805 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:22:29,559 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You’re very welcome. I’m grateful we had this time together. Remember, healing doesn’t have to be fast or complete — just showing up with kindness, even on hard days, is already a strength. Take care, and I’ll be here whenever you need to talk again.
2026-03-02 02:22:29,559 - src.sim - INFO - Conversation ended.
2026-03-02 02:22:29,560 - src.sim - INFO - Conversation dumped to data_out/new_1/conversation_profile8_20260302T012229.json
