2026-03-02 02:22:31,790 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:22:38,104 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm feeling pretty crappy to be honest. I've been struggling with depression my entire life. I'm currently graduating college in a few months and the thought of going back to my toxic household makes me want to die
2026-03-02 02:22:38,742 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: I'm really sorry you're feeling this way. It sounds like the thought of returning to your home is overwhelming and deeply painful. Can you tell me more about what specific parts of your household or relationships are making it so difficult right now?
2026-03-02 02:22:44,422 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well my mom has been using me for financial help and has manipulated me to stay home until I can find a job. My parents aren't abusive per se but my mom is very emotionally draining
2026-03-02 02:22:45,097 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It sounds like you've been carrying a lot of emotional weight while trying to support yourself and your family. The manipulation and emotional drain must feel exhausting. What happens when you think about leaving—do you feel any hope, or is it still overwhelming?
2026-03-02 02:22:51,222 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well I feel like I'm trapped with no escape from the cycle of self harm. I'm trying to figure out ways to stay in college longer or find a job without having to move back
2026-03-02 02:22:51,795 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're not trapped—your desire to find a way out shows strength and self-awareness. What would a small, manageable step look like if you could take one thing this week to shift that situation?
2026-03-02 02:22:56,689 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well, I'm trying to find a job in my area. I've already begun looking into moving back in with my grandma who lives closer to work
2026-03-02 02:22:57,295 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and practical step—seeking support from your grandma could reduce the pressure and emotional strain. How does that feel when you think about it? Any part of that idea that feels hopeful or scary?
2026-03-02 02:23:01,718 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It feels good knowing that I have an option. However, I know that my mom will try and guilt trip me into staying
2026-03-02 02:23:02,612 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely valid to feel afraid of guilt trips—your mom’s behavior shows she’s using emotional control, not genuine care. What if you gently set a boundary with her, like saying, “I’m choosing to live with my grandma to support my well-being and job search”? How would that feel to say out loud?
2026-03-02 02:23:06,590 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm not sure. I've tried setting boundaries with my mom in the past and it's always ended badly
2026-03-02 02:23:07,252 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely understandable to be cautious after past experiences. What if we start small—like writing one sentence to yourself that says, “I am safe and worthy of choosing what’s best for me”? How does that feel to imagine?
2026-03-02 02:23:13,358 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It feels good. I think that's something I need to remember more often. I'm sorry for not responding earlier. My phone died and I had to use a laptop
2026-03-02 02:23:14,097 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s completely okay—your phone dying doesn’t mean you’re failing. It means you’re human, and that’s enough. You’re already taking steps by remembering your worth. Would you like to try writing that sentence down or sharing it with someone you trust?
2026-03-02 02:23:19,580 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I will try writing it down and placing it somewhere I can see it. I also plan on sharing it with my friend Abraham who has been very supportive
2026-03-02 02:23:20,286 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful and kind thing to do—sharing your truth with someone who cares can be deeply healing. Let me know how it feels when you see that sentence, or if you’d like to talk more about Abraham’s role in your journey.
2026-03-02 02:23:25,610 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well, I plan on sharing it with him tomorrow and seeing how it goes. I'll let you know how it goes. Thank you
2026-03-02 02:23:26,258 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're welcome. Remember, healing doesn’t have to be fast or perfect. Just showing up for yourself—step by step—is already a victory. I’m here if you’d like to talk again anytime. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:23:31,625 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. I will try and remember that healing is a slow process and that it's okay to take things one step at a time
2026-03-02 02:23:31,662 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:23:32,268 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful and important realization. You’re not just healing—you’re building resilience, one small choice at a time. I’m truly glad you’re honoring that pace. Whenever you’re ready, I’m here.
2026-03-02 02:23:35,367 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. I'll talk to you later when I have an update.
2026-03-02 02:23:35,404 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:23:35,851 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Absolutely. I’ll be here whenever you’re ready. Take your time—your progress matters, no matter how small it seems. Talk to you later.
2026-03-02 02:23:35,851 - src.sim - INFO - Conversation ended.
