2026-03-02 02:27:43,212 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:27:45,662 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hello. I'm feeling very sad today. I've been lonely since I broke up with my boyfriend.
2026-03-02 02:27:46,121 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like the breakup has left a deep sense of loneliness. What does that sadness feel like in your body or mind right now?
2026-03-02 02:27:48,979 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel like I'm going to cry. I keep thinking about how I made a mistake by breaking up.
2026-03-02 02:27:49,421 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s common to feel regret after a breakup, especially when you’re grieving the connection. What specific thought about making a mistake comes to mind most often?
2026-03-02 02:27:52,949 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I know I need to get him back, but I know I shouldn't have broken up with him because he was horrible to me.
2026-03-02 02:27:53,567 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It sounds like you're carrying a lot of guilt and confusion—wanting to fix things, but also recognizing the pain he caused. What would it mean to you to truly let go of that need to get him back?
2026-03-02 02:27:56,889 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I would feel okay I guess. But I feel like I need him back because I was important when I was with him.
2026-03-02 02:27:57,441 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful feeling—your worth was tied to your relationship with him. What would it look like if you started seeing your value as something that exists even when you’re not with him?
2026-03-02 02:27:59,730 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel like I need to get him back to feel important and loved.
2026-03-02 02:28:00,245 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s clear that your sense of self-worth has been deeply connected to that relationship. What small step might help you begin to see yourself as valuable outside of being with him?
2026-03-02 02:28:04,847 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I need to just try and get him back and make it work. I feel like he is the only one who can make me feel loved and important.
2026-03-02 02:28:05,405 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It sounds like you’re holding on to the hope that he can restore what you’ve lost. What would it mean to you if you could love and value yourself even without him in your life?
2026-03-02 02:28:07,738 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel like it will never happen and I'll always feel like this.
2026-03-02 02:28:08,274 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s okay to feel that way—this sadness and uncertainty are part of healing. What small thing could you do today to care for yourself, even if it doesn’t involve him?
2026-03-02 02:28:10,326 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could hang out with my friends and not think about him.
2026-03-02 02:28:10,916 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a gentle and meaningful step. Taking time with friends can help you reconnect with yourself and others without relying on him. How would that feel if you tried it, even for just one hour today?
2026-03-02 02:28:14,218 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think it will make me feel a little better and I'll have something to look forward to.
2026-03-02 02:28:14,832 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a hopeful shift—focusing on something you can control, rather than waiting for someone else to change things. Would you like to plan a small moment with a friend tomorrow, just to try it out?
2026-03-02 02:28:16,101 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, that would be nice.
2026-03-02 02:28:16,509 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great. Starting small can build confidence over time. What time or day would work best for you to connect with a friend tomorrow?
2026-03-02 02:28:18,211 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Tuesday afternoon or Thursday evening would be best.
2026-03-02 02:28:18,830 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Tuesday afternoon or Thursday evening—those are both good choices. Let’s say you pick one and just show up, even if it’s just to sit, talk, or do something simple. How does that feel?
2026-03-02 02:28:22,086 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It feels nice to have something to look forward to and it will make me feel better.
2026-03-02 02:28:22,695 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful feeling—looking forward to something small, meaningful, and within your control. It’s a quiet but strong step toward healing. Let me know how it goes when you meet with your friend.
2026-03-02 02:28:25,816 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you so much for listening and helping me. I feel a little better already.
2026-03-02 02:28:26,605 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're welcome. It's wonderful that you're taking this step, even just by being open to change. Remember, healing isn’t about fixing everything—it’s about showing up for yourself, one small moment at a time. I’m here if you’d like to talk again.
2026-03-02 02:28:29,743 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you again. I'll let you know how my meeting with my friend goes.
2026-03-02 02:28:29,775 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:28:30,373 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and brave thing to do. Your willingness to try, even with uncertainty, is already a form of strength. I’ll be here when you’re ready to share how it went. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:28:32,672 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you so much. I feel a little better already.
2026-03-02 02:28:32,705 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:28:33,371 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're welcome. It’s beautiful that you’re noticing the small shifts—just showing up for yourself is a powerful act of self-care. I’m glad you feel a little better. Let me know when you’d like to talk again.
