2026-03-02 02:28:35,643 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:28:39,286 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm feeling a little anxious, because I have a big test in college coming up. I think my future depends on it, so that's giving me a lot of anxiety
2026-03-02 02:28:39,904 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That makes sense—feeling anxious about a big test, especially when it feels like it could impact your future, is very understandable. What’s one specific thought or worry about the test that’s standing out to you right now?
2026-03-02 02:28:42,416 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Even though I've been studying for weeks, I still feel like I'm going to freeze up and not pass.
2026-03-02 02:28:43,019 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s common to feel that way even with preparation—your fear of failing can feel overwhelming. What evidence do you have that you might actually freeze up during the test? And what evidence suggests you could do well instead?
2026-03-02 02:28:48,903 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well, I've studied really hard and prepared well, so I think I have the evidence that I could do well. But, I've never taken a test like this before, so that's the fear that I'll freeze up
2026-03-02 02:28:49,887 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a strong point—your preparation gives you confidence, and that’s valid. The fact that this is your first time facing such a high-stakes test might amplify the fear of the unknown. What if you tried a small experiment: take a practice test under timed conditions and notice how you actually feel during it? Would that help you see what’s really happening?
2026-03-02 02:28:53,322 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that's a great idea. That might actually help me reduce my anxiety because I'll know how I feel under timed conditions
2026-03-02 02:28:53,799 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great—taking a practice test is a practical and kind way to build confidence. What would be the first step you’d take tomorrow to start that practice session?
2026-03-02 02:28:56,791 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I would look up where I can get the practice tests and then start doing them as soon as possible
2026-03-02 02:28:57,453 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a clear and actionable plan. Starting small—like finding one practice test today—can make the process feel less overwhelming. How would you feel if you completed just one practice test this week, even if it wasn’t perfect?
2026-03-02 02:29:00,483 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that would actually give me a lot of confidence to know that I was able to complete it
2026-03-02 02:29:01,122 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful mindset shift—focusing on completion rather than perfection can reduce pressure. Every step you take builds real evidence of your ability. Would you like to pick a specific subject or type of test to start with?
2026-03-02 02:29:03,166 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll start with math because that's my weakest area
2026-03-02 02:29:03,879 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Starting with math is a smart choice—addressing your weakest area first can build momentum and confidence. What would you do if you found a practice math problem you couldn’t solve right away? Would you try it again later, or look up the solution?
2026-03-02 02:29:07,099 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I would try to look up the solution because that's how I've been learning in the past
2026-03-02 02:29:07,751 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a healthy approach—learning from solutions helps you understand concepts without feeling stuck. What if you tried solving one problem first, then checked the solution only after you’ve given it your best effort? Would that feel more manageable?
2026-03-02 02:29:11,207 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that's a great idea because I feel like I have been relying too heavily on looking up solutions
2026-03-02 02:29:11,965 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're recognizing a pattern that’s important—balancing effort with learning helps build real skill. Trying one problem first, then checking the solution, gives you both practice and insight. How would you feel if you completed that first problem and then reviewed it with the solution?
2026-03-02 02:29:16,722 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that would actually make me feel really good about myself because I know I put in the effort to try and then learn from it
2026-03-02 02:29:17,403 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful feeling—effort and reflection build confidence over time. You’re not just preparing for a test; you’re building resilience and self-trust. Would you like to pick a specific math topic to start with tomorrow?
2026-03-02 02:29:20,658 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll start with linear algebra because that's what's coming up on the test
2026-03-02 02:29:21,290 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Linear algebra is a great starting point—it’s common in college tests and builds on foundational skills. What’s one small concept or topic within linear algebra that feels most unfamiliar to you? That could be your first focus.
2026-03-02 02:29:24,760 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll start with eigenvalues because that's what we've been learning in class recently
2026-03-02 02:29:25,373 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Eigenvalues are a key topic—starting there makes sense given your recent studies. What’s one thing you already know about eigenvalues that gives you some sense of confidence? Even small pieces help build momentum.
2026-03-02 02:29:29,729 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I know how to find them and what they represent, so that gives me some confidence that I can do it
2026-03-02 02:29:30,504 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a solid foundation—knowing how to find eigenvalues and what they represent means you already have a clear path forward. What if you tried solving one simple eigenvalue problem today, step by step, and then reviewed your solution afterward? Would that feel manageable?
2026-03-02 02:29:35,808 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that's a great idea because I feel like I've been putting off doing them because I feel like I don't know where to start
2026-03-02 02:29:36,641 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're absolutely right—starting small removes the pressure of “doing it all.” Taking one step today builds confidence and shows you can begin. What’s the first step you’d take to solve a simple eigenvalue problem? For example, writing down the matrix or setting up the characteristic equation?
2026-03-02 02:29:42,529 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think the first step I'd take would be to write down the matrix because I feel like that's where I've been getting stuck in the past
2026-03-02 02:29:43,469 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a smart and practical first step—writing down the matrix clears the way for the rest. Many people hesitate because they see the matrix as a complex or intimidating part, but it’s just the starting point. Would you like to pick a simple 2x2 matrix from class notes or a textbook to try with that?
2026-03-02 02:29:48,488 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll start with a simple 2x2 matrix because I feel like that's where I've been getting stuck
2026-03-02 02:29:49,325 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: A 2x2 matrix is an excellent choice—simple enough to focus on, but meaningful for learning eigenvalues. What if you picked a matrix from your class notes and wrote it down step by step, just to get comfortable with the format? Would that feel doable today?
2026-03-02 02:29:52,821 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that's a great idea. I think I will start doing that right away
2026-03-02 02:29:53,566 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great—starting right now builds momentum and turns anxiety into action. Remember, you don’t need to get it perfect; just showing up and taking that first step is a win. How does it feel when you’re actually writing down the matrix?
2026-03-02 02:29:57,524 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel really good about it because I'm actually doing something instead of just worrying about it
2026-03-02 02:29:58,245 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s exactly it—action replaces worry. Feeling good about doing something small is a powerful shift. What happens next after you write down the matrix? Do you feel ready to move to the next step, like finding the characteristic equation?
2026-03-02 02:30:04,388 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'm ready to move on because I feel like writing down the matrix is where I've been getting stuck in the past
2026-03-02 02:30:05,136 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a key breakthrough—seeing the matrix as a manageable first step removes the barrier you’ve been facing. What would you do next after writing it down? For example, setting up the characteristic equation? Would you like to try that together?
2026-03-02 02:30:09,576 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll try to do that next because I feel like I understand it now.
2026-03-02 02:30:10,339 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great choice—moving forward step by step builds confidence and clarity. Setting up the characteristic equation is a natural next step. What would you do if you found a small mistake while solving it? Would you correct it and try again, or move on?
2026-03-02 02:30:17,059 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I would try to correct it and then move on because I feel like that's how I've been learning in the past
2026-03-02 02:30:17,826 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a strong and consistent learning habit—correcting mistakes and moving forward helps build both accuracy and confidence. What if you took a moment to pause after correcting it, and then simply notice how you feel? Did the process feel different than before?
2026-03-02 02:30:23,347 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel like it feels different because I feel like I understand it now and I'm not just guessing.
2026-03-02 02:30:24,081 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful shift—feeling in control, not guessing, is a powerful sense of clarity. You’re not just studying; you’re building understanding through action and reflection. How would you like to continue with this process tomorrow?
2026-03-02 02:30:28,633 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll try to do some more practice problems tomorrow and see how I feel
2026-03-02 02:30:29,322 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a great plan—consistent, small practice builds confidence over time. What would you do if you found a problem you didn’t understand right away? Would you pause, try again, or look it up?
2026-03-02 02:30:34,909 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I would try to do it again and then look it up if I'm still having trouble
2026-03-02 02:30:35,729 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a balanced and effective approach—giving yourself time to try first builds effort and resilience. Each attempt, even if it doesn’t go perfectly, adds to your understanding. How would you feel if you completed one problem today and then reflected on it afterward?
2026-03-02 02:30:41,614 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll feel really good about myself because I know I put in the effort and tried my best
2026-03-02 02:30:42,618 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful and healthy self-view—valuing effort over perfection strengthens your confidence and self-trust. Every time you try, you’re not just preparing for the test; you’re building a more resilient, capable version of yourself. Would you like to set a small goal for tomorrow, like completing one problem and reflecting on it?
2026-03-02 02:30:47,747 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that's a great goal. I think I'll try to do that tomorrow.
2026-03-02 02:30:48,662 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a strong, realistic goal—small, consistent steps lead to big changes over time. You’re already showing up with care and intention. How would you like to check in with yourself after you complete that one problem tomorrow? For example, just saying, “I tried, I learned something”?
2026-03-02 02:30:55,511 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll try to say something like that to myself because it'll help me remember that I put in the effort
2026-03-02 02:30:56,478 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful practice—repeating a simple affirmation like that reinforces your effort and self-worth. It becomes a mental anchor, reminding you that progress isn’t about perfection, but about showing up. Thank you for being so open and thoughtful. Would you like to keep checking in like this as you go forward?
2026-03-02 02:31:00,393 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that would be great. Thank you for your help.
2026-03-02 02:31:01,303 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. It's wonderful that you're taking these small, meaningful steps—each one builds confidence and clarity. If you'd like to check in again anytime, even just to reflect on how you felt during the day, I'm here. Take care, and keep showing up.
2026-03-02 02:31:05,917 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you so much. I really appreciate it. Have a great day.
2026-03-02 02:31:05,955 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:31:06,692 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. It's been a pleasure walking this journey with you. Remember, your effort matters more than any single outcome. Take care, and I hope your day brings you peace and small wins. You've got this.
2026-03-02 02:31:09,323 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. I appreciate your help.
2026-03-02 02:31:09,360 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:31:10,060 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. Thank you for being open and honest—your willingness to reflect and take action is already a sign of strength. Take care, and remember to return when you're ready. You've got this.
