2026-03-02 02:31:12,297 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:31:15,848 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hello.  I found out that my boyfriend cheated on me with my best friend a year ago.  I'm angry and feel like everything in my life is wrong.
2026-03-02 02:31:16,429 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like you're carrying a lot of pain right now. Can you tell me more about how you first found out, and what part of that experience feels most difficult for you?
2026-03-02 02:31:20,905 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I didn't know about the infidelity until my boyfriend and I were going to get engaged and I found out about the affair.  I was shocked and felt so betrayed.
2026-03-02 02:31:21,446 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That must have been incredibly painful—being betrayed by someone you trusted, especially someone you shared a deep connection with. What part of that moment or what comes after it feels most overwhelming right now?
2026-03-02 02:31:24,993 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm not sure if I should still be in the relationship.  My boyfriend wants me to forgive and forget but I can't.
2026-03-02 02:31:25,668 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely valid to feel that you can’t forgive and forget—your pain is real and important. What would it look like for you to take a step toward deciding whether to stay in the relationship, or if you need space or change?
2026-03-02 02:31:30,716 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I need to consider my options.  I know another guy who has been interested in me for over a year.  I never considered him because I have been with my boyfriend for so long.
2026-03-02 02:31:31,471 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s meaningful that you’ve been open to someone else for a long time, even if you didn’t act on it. What do you think about how your feelings for this other person might reflect what you’re really seeking right now—trust, safety, or something else?
2026-03-02 02:31:34,488 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel like I might have wasted time with the wrong person.  I should consider other options.
2026-03-02 02:31:35,199 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s okay to feel that way—recognizing a pattern or a mismatch can be a powerful first step. What would it mean for you to explore this new possibility, and what might be one small step you could take to begin that exploration without pressure?
2026-03-02 02:31:40,735 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel anxious because my life is very planned and I don't know what to do.  I always have a plan for my life and right now my life feels out of control.
2026-03-02 02:31:41,413 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It makes sense that a sudden shift like this would feel like chaos—especially when you’ve always relied on plans to feel secure. What would a small, manageable step look like that could help you regain a sense of control without overwhelming you?
2026-03-02 02:31:45,150 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe talking to the other guy or maybe breaking up with my boyfriend.  I don't know which one is right.
2026-03-02 02:31:45,756 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s okay to feel uncertain—both options carry weight and emotion. What would it feel like to explore talking to the other guy, and what might happen if you decided to break up with your boyfriend first?
2026-03-02 02:31:50,172 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm sad that I'm even considering breaking up with my boyfriend.  He is so sincere and wants to work on our relationship.
2026-03-02 02:31:50,856 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sadness makes sense—your loyalty and care for him are important, and it’s not easy to let go of someone who’s sincere. What part of your relationship do you still value, even if you’re feeling unsure about the future?
2026-03-02 02:31:54,442 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I still love him but I'm not sure if he is the right person for me.
2026-03-02 02:31:55,216 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s powerful that you’re aware of your love and your doubts at the same time. What would it mean for you to take time to reflect on what "the right person" might look like—not just in terms of actions, but in how they meet your emotional needs?
2026-03-02 02:31:59,662 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I need to think about it more.  I have so many thoughts and I can't make sense of them.
2026-03-02 02:32:00,331 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely normal to feel overwhelmed when so many emotions are moving at once. What if you started by writing down just one thought or feeling each day—no pressure, just to help you see patterns without getting lost in them?
2026-03-02 02:32:04,839 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I will try writing down my thoughts.  Maybe I can gain more clarity by putting my thoughts down on paper.
2026-03-02 02:32:05,672 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and gentle first step. Starting small—just one thought a day—can help you see what’s truly on your mind without judgment. How would you like to begin? For example, could you write about one feeling from today, or one question you’ve been asking yourself?
2026-03-02 02:32:11,136 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I can try writing about one feeling from today.  Maybe it will help me feel better and get my mind off of everything that's going on.
2026-03-02 02:32:11,853 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds like a kind and grounding choice. Writing about one feeling can help you notice what’s truly present without trying to fix it. Would you like to try it now, or would you prefer to set a time to do it together later?
2026-03-02 02:32:17,108 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I can try writing about one feeling right now.  Maybe it will help me get my mind off of things and help me feel better.
2026-03-02 02:32:17,895 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great—let’s start with just one feeling. Take a moment to notice what’s on your mind right now. You don’t need to explain it or make it perfect. Just say one word or short phrase that captures how you’re feeling. Ready when you are.
2026-03-02 02:32:19,992 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm ready.  I'm feeling sad.
2026-03-02 02:32:20,917 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. Sadness is a valid and important emotion—especially when you’re carrying so much. Just feeling it, without needing to change it, can be a step toward peace. Would you like to write a short sentence about that sadness, or would you prefer to pause and come back to it later?
2026-03-02 02:32:26,521 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I will pause and come back to it later.  I need to think about it more and get my mind off of it.
2026-03-02 02:32:27,164 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s completely okay. Taking space to rest is part of healing. You’re not rushing yourself—just being present with your emotions. When you're ready, we can return to it together. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:32:29,883 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you for your help today.  I feel better already.
2026-03-02 02:32:29,920 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:32:30,681 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: I'm glad to hear that—just feeling better, even a little, is a meaningful step. Remember, you don’t have to have all the answers today. Coming back to this with kindness is enough. When you’re ready, we can continue. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:32:33,807 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you.  I feel better and I'm not as sad.
2026-03-02 02:32:33,844 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:32:34,562 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s wonderful to hear. It’s okay to move slowly and honor your own pace. You’re doing something important by allowing yourself to feel and reflect. If you ever want to talk more or return to this, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:32:34,562 - src.sim - INFO - Conversation ended.
