2026-03-02 02:39:22,400 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:39:26,500 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I am sad today. I am not sure if I can ever feel okay again. I am worried I will never have a girlfriend again. I feel lost.
2026-03-02 02:39:27,045 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like you're carrying a lot of weight right now. Can you tell me more about what specifically makes you feel lost or afraid about never having a girlfriend again?
2026-03-02 02:39:32,161 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: My girlfriend died by suicide 5 months ago. I am still grieving. I feel guilty that she is not here. I wonder if I could have done things differently so she would still be here.
2026-03-02 02:39:32,924 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: I’m truly sorry you’re going through this. It’s completely understandable to feel guilt and sadness when someone you love has passed. What would it mean to you if you could think of one small thing you did or didn’t do that might have made a difference—without judging yourself?
2026-03-02 02:39:37,295 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I don’t think I can think of anything. I do feel like if I could have shown her love in another way, she might still be here.
2026-03-02 02:39:38,057 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s okay to feel that way—love is deeply meaningful, and your care for her matters. What if we explored one small thing you did that showed her you cared, even in the way you were able to? That might help you see her presence in a different light.
2026-03-02 02:39:42,509 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I did care for her deeply. I feel like I was doing things right. I am still trying to figure out how to go on without her.
2026-03-02 02:39:43,377 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful and honest reflection. You’ve already shown deep love and care—those are not just things you did, they’re part of who you are now. What would it mean to you, in this moment, if you allowed yourself to gently notice one small thing you’ve been doing that honors her presence?
2026-03-02 02:39:46,723 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I could do that. It would be hard though. I wish I could talk to her again.
2026-03-02 02:39:47,524 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s natural to wish you could speak to her again—it feels like a way to heal. What if we imagine a simple way you could connect with her now, not to change the past, but to feel her presence in a gentle, peaceful way? How might that look to you?
2026-03-02 02:39:52,709 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds nice. I would like that. I think that would help. I would like to honor her memory by keeping her in my life in some way.
2026-03-02 02:39:53,513 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful intention—honoring her by keeping her in your life means you’re not letting go, you’re carrying her love forward. What might a small, quiet act be that feels meaningful to you, like writing a note, planting a tree, or simply remembering her smile?
2026-03-02 02:39:58,402 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think a quiet act would be remembering her smile. I could do that everyday. I could smile too when I think of her smile.
2026-03-02 02:39:59,268 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a gentle and powerful way to carry her with you. Remembering her smile—especially when you choose to smile back—creates a small, daily connection. How would you feel if you began with just one moment each day, like noticing a light in the morning or a quiet moment when you feel safe?
2026-03-02 02:40:03,897 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That would be great. I think I could do that. That would make me feel better. I would feel connected to her.
2026-03-02 02:40:04,585 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful step. By choosing to smile and remember her, you’re not just honoring her—you’re creating a space where love continues to live in you. How would you like to start this small practice today? Just one moment?
2026-03-02 02:40:08,397 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could do that now. I could smile thinking about her smile. That would make me feel better.
2026-03-02 02:40:09,078 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful choice. Taking that moment now—smiling, remembering—creates a quiet bridge between you and her. Would you like to keep checking in with that feeling tomorrow, just to see how it feels in your heart?
2026-03-02 02:40:13,482 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That would be great. I think I will do that. Thank you for helping me find a way to honor her.
2026-03-02 02:40:14,226 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're welcome. Thank you for trusting me with this. Your kindness in choosing to remember her, even in small ways, is a form of love that keeps her close. I’m here if you’d like to share more or return when you’re ready.
2026-03-02 02:40:17,059 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. I feel better. I will talk to you again tomorrow.
2026-03-02 02:40:17,096 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:40:17,762 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds like a meaningful next step. I’m glad a small moment of connection helped you feel a little lighter. Take care of yourself tomorrow—just being present with that smile might be enough. I’ll be here when you’re ready.
2026-03-02 02:40:20,995 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. I feel lighter. I think that will help me a lot.
2026-03-02 02:40:21,032 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:40:21,853 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful feeling—lightness comes from small, honest moments like this. Thank you for sharing your truth. I’m glad it helped. When you’re ready, we can continue exploring how to carry her love forward in ways that feel true to you. You’re not alone in this.
