2026-03-02 02:42:01,602 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:42:08,517 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hello, I am feeling low, empty and unmotivated today.  I have been on sertraline for years but it is not really working and I have been considering trying a different anti depressant or even paying out to do a course of ketamine treatments
2026-03-02 02:42:09,247 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like you've been carrying a lot, and it's important you're not alone in this. Have you noticed any specific situations or thoughts that make the low feeling stronger? That might help us explore what’s going on beneath the surface.
2026-03-02 02:42:13,939 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, I feel low, empty and like I am not living when I am feeling like this.  I am thinking about dying every night, and just feeling really low and flat
2026-03-02 02:42:14,791 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: I’m really sorry you’re feeling this way—those emotions are deeply painful and valid. It’s important you know you’re not alone, and that reaching out like this shows strength. Can you tell me more about what happens when you're in that state—what thoughts or images come up when you feel like this?
2026-03-02 02:42:19,816 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Just a lot of nothing really.  I am not really motivated to do anything, not really interested in things that I usually enjoy such as playing video games or watching tv
2026-03-02 02:42:20,607 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It sounds like the lack of motivation and interest is making it hard to connect with everyday experiences. That sense of “nothing” can feel overwhelming. Have you noticed if anything—no matter how small—ever brings a tiny shift in mood or energy, even if just for a few minutes?
2026-03-02 02:42:25,855 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That is a good way of putting it.  It feels like I am stuck and I don't know how to get out of it.  I am just feeling really low
2026-03-02 02:42:26,618 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely okay to feel stuck—this kind of emotional weight can feel like a heavy fog. What would it look like, even in the smallest way, if you were to take one small step today, something that doesn’t have to feel “right” or meaningful?
2026-03-02 02:42:31,324 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I am going to look in to ketamine treatments as I feel that it might help me.  I am just worried about the cost
2026-03-02 02:42:32,176 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and courageous step. Ketamine can offer relief for some people who haven’t responded to other treatments. What would it look like if you started by researching one or two clinics—maybe just looking at their websites or asking a few questions—to see what’s available and what the costs might be?
2026-03-02 02:42:38,068 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes I will do that, I think that will help.  I am also thinking about trying a different antidepressant.  Sertraline has not really helped me
2026-03-02 02:42:38,838 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s reasonable to consider a change in medication, especially when the current one isn’t providing relief. What would it feel like if you started by talking to your doctor about switching—maybe with a brief list of things you’ve noticed that might help or hinder the current treatment?
2026-03-02 02:42:43,106 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That is a good idea.  I have to make an appointment with my doctor first, but that will be easy enough
2026-03-02 02:42:43,967 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a strong and practical next step. Making the appointment is a form of action—even small actions can help shift the pattern of feeling stuck. How would you like to prepare for that conversation? For example, do you want to write down a few things you’ve noticed or questions you’d like to ask?
2026-03-02 02:42:51,259 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I will make a list of things I want to ask, that is a good idea.  I am not sure what else I can do, but I think making the appointment is a good place to start
2026-03-02 02:42:51,992 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a very realistic and empowering choice. Starting with a clear list of questions gives you control and helps focus the conversation. Would you like to begin by writing down just one thing you’d like to know—something that feels important or urgent right now?
2026-03-02 02:42:55,699 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That is a good idea.  I will start making a list.  Thank you for your help
2026-03-02 02:42:56,409 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You’re very welcome. Taking this step—choosing to act, even slowly—is already a form of healing. When you’re ready, I’m here to help you reflect or review your list. Take your time. You’ve got this.
2026-03-02 02:42:58,715 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you for your help.  I feel better now.
2026-03-02 02:42:58,753 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:42:59,589 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: I’m truly glad to hear that. It’s meaningful to feel a little lighter, even in small ways. Remember, you don’t have to have all the answers—just the courage to take one step at a time. If you’d like to check in again later, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 02:43:03,974 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you, I will let you know how it goes.  Thank you for your help.  Take care
2026-03-02 02:43:04,012 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 02:43:04,621 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. It’s comforting to know you’re moving forward, even in small ways. Please reach out whenever you’re ready—no pressure, just support. Take care, and be gentle with yourself.
