2026-03-02 02:59:15,791 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 02:59:19,930 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: hello, i'm feeling anxious about an upcoming college test, believe my future depends on doing well.  I've been studying hard for weeks but feel like I will freeze up when I take the test
2026-03-02 02:59:20,646 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It sounds like you're carrying a lot of weight with this test—your anxiety is likely tied to how much it feels like your future depends on it. What happens when you notice yourself feeling anxious right before the test? Do you notice any specific thoughts that come up?
2026-03-02 02:59:25,661 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I have trouble sleeping lately as my mind keeps racing about how I will do on the test.  I've been studying for weeks and feel like I know the material but still fear that I won't do well
2026-03-02 02:59:26,204 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s common for intense focus on performance to create a cycle of worry and sleep disruption. When you notice your mind racing, what does that thought usually look like—something specific you imagine happening?
2026-03-02 02:59:28,705 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I usually imagine myself doing poorly on the test, which causes me to feel more anxious about it
2026-03-02 02:59:29,483 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful thought—imagining failure can actually increase anxiety. What if we looked at that thought: "I’ll do poorly" — what evidence supports it, and what evidence might challenge it? Try to name one thing that supports it and one thing that contradicts it.
2026-03-02 02:59:35,007 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think the thought is supported by the fact that I feel anxious about it and fear of not knowing the material.  A thing that contradicts it is that I have studied for weeks and feel confident about the material
2026-03-02 02:59:35,753 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great insight—your awareness of both the fear and your confidence is a strong starting point. What would happen if you tried to test that thought in a small way, like taking a short practice test or reviewing one topic without worrying about the outcome? How might that feel?
2026-03-02 02:59:40,495 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that might help alleviate some of the anxiety about the test.  I feel like my confidence in the material is stronger than my fear of not doing well
2026-03-02 02:59:41,220 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful shift—recognizing your confidence as stronger than your fear is a key step. What would happen if you tried doing just one small thing today to test that confidence, like reviewing a topic you’re most comfortable with, without judging the outcome?
2026-03-02 02:59:43,247 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I will do that today, thank you for your help
2026-03-02 02:59:43,918 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and brave step. Remember, you’re not expected to be perfect—just to take one small action toward building trust in yourself. How did that small practice feel when you did it? I’d love to hear.
2026-03-02 02:59:46,247 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That felt helpful, I think that will help me reduce my anxiety a bit
2026-03-02 02:59:46,904 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful outcome—your confidence is growing, even in small ways. What might happen if you noticed yourself feeling calm or in control during the next study session? Try to catch that feeling and give yourself credit for it.
2026-03-02 02:59:49,892 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that would be very empowering, feeling in control and calm would definitely help my anxiety
2026-03-02 02:59:50,552 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Exactly—feeling in control is a powerful shift. Next time you notice anxiety rising, pause and gently ask yourself: "What small step can I take right now to feel more grounded?" That question can become a quiet anchor.
2026-03-02 02:59:53,081 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I will definitely try that, thank you for your help today!
2026-03-02 02:59:53,777 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. It’s wonderful that you’re taking these small, intentional steps—each one builds strength and calm over time. How do you think you’ll feel when you return to the test, knowing you’ve already practiced trusting yourself?
2026-03-02 02:59:58,024 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I will feel a lot calmer and more confident that I know the material, I appreciate your help today!
2026-03-02 02:59:58,057 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 02:59:58,738 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful outcome—confidence built through action, not just expectation. You’ve already shown great self-awareness and courage. If you ever want to reflect on how the test went or how your mindset changed, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 03:00:00,799 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you! I hope you have a great day!
2026-03-02 03:00:00,832 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
