2026-03-02 03:02:31,782 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 03:02:35,834 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hello, i'm not sure where to start really. But I suppose my recent relationship with this girl has made me realize how much I like her, but it seems it's not mutual
2026-03-02 03:02:36,330 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds like a lot to process. Can you tell me more about how you first noticed that you liked her? Was there a specific moment or behavior that stood out to you?
2026-03-02 03:02:39,950 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well I've had feelings for her for a few years now, but we've recently started talking and she thought of me as just a friend
2026-03-02 03:02:40,503 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s interesting that the feelings have grown over time, even though she’s seen you as a friend. What does that make you feel—disappointed, confused, or maybe even a little sad?
2026-03-02 03:02:43,796 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I suppose I'm a bit confused. I don't really understand why she thought of me as just a friend all this time
2026-03-02 03:02:44,360 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a valid question. Sometimes people don’t express their feelings clearly, even when they’re aware of them. What do you think might have been unspoken or unexpressed on her part?
2026-03-02 03:02:48,538 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe she just wasn't ready to express her feelings at that time? She told me recently she had feelings for my best friend, which kind of crushed me.
2026-03-02 03:02:49,094 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s understandable that that news would feel disappointing—especially when you’ve cared for her over years. How do you think that might have affected your sense of her being open or honest with you?
2026-03-02 03:02:54,404 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well, I suppose I kind of put my feelings aside and tried to be happy for her. I told her my friend was a great choice, and tried to keep my head up
2026-03-02 03:02:54,902 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That shows a lot of emotional strength—choosing to support her while still holding your own feelings. How do you think that decision made you feel, in the moment?
2026-03-02 03:02:58,666 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I suppose it was better than crying, so I'm not sure it was the best decision, but it was a tough one
2026-03-02 03:02:59,217 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely okay to feel uncertain—especially when your heart is involved. What would it feel like if you were to explore your feelings a little more, without pressure to act right now?
2026-03-02 03:03:03,014 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I would feel a lot better if I could tell her how I feel, maybe she would feel the same way.
2026-03-02 03:03:03,472 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful and honest step. What might be one small way you could begin sharing your feelings with her—something that feels safe and manageable?
2026-03-02 03:03:08,305 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe I could just ask her what her intentions were when we first started talking. I suppose I ignored a few signs she didn't really want a relationship
2026-03-02 03:03:08,735 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and gentle approach. How might you feel if you asked her about her intentions—without expecting an immediate answer or change?
2026-03-02 03:03:12,673 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I would feel better knowing, but it might still hurt to know that she just didn't have feelings for me
2026-03-02 03:03:13,142 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely normal to feel both hope and sadness when exploring something that’s been meaningful. What would help you carry that pain with a little more peace?
2026-03-02 03:03:17,222 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I suppose I could just try to tell her how I feel about her. Maybe she will feel the same way.
2026-03-02 03:03:17,756 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a brave and kind choice. What if you started by sharing your feelings in a way that’s gentle and open—without expecting anything in return? How might that feel?
2026-03-02 03:03:23,206 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It might feel good to just tell her how I feel. Maybe I could ask my friend how he feels about her too, he seems to like her too
2026-03-02 03:03:23,709 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds like a thoughtful and balanced way to move forward. How might sharing your feelings with her help you feel more at peace, even if the outcome is different?
2026-03-02 03:03:28,212 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think just telling her how I feel would help me get closure. I don't like not knowing how someone else feels.
2026-03-02 03:03:28,754 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful and mature desire—for closure, for understanding. What if you shared your feelings in a way that felt honest, but also respected her space and time to respond?
2026-03-02 03:03:31,362 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe I could just ask her out again and explain how I feel.
2026-03-02 03:03:31,807 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That could be a meaningful step—asking her out while gently sharing your feelings. How might you imagine that conversation going, in your mind?
2026-03-02 03:03:36,387 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I would feel a lot better knowing either way. Maybe I'll just ask her out again and see what happens.
2026-03-02 03:03:36,975 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds like a thoughtful and courageous choice. It’s okay to go forward with hope, even when the outcome is uncertain. How will you care for yourself if things don’t go as hoped?
2026-03-02 03:03:42,463 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll just have to try and find someone else to spend time with. Maybe my friend will ask me to hang out with him and her
2026-03-02 03:03:43,080 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a gentle and realistic way to move forward. Spending time with your friend and her could help you grow in new ways—without pressure. How do you think that might feel, in the moment?
2026-03-02 03:03:46,693 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It might feel a little weird, but it's better than just sitting alone all the time
2026-03-02 03:03:47,277 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely normal to feel a bit uneasy when stepping into new connections. What might help you feel more comfortable in that space—something small, like a shared activity or a simple conversation?
2026-03-02 03:03:50,331 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I suppose I could just ask them if they want to do something together.
2026-03-02 03:03:50,978 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a natural and kind next step. Starting with a simple invitation can open the door to new experiences without pressure. How do you think it might feel if you said it with a light, open-hearted tone?
2026-03-02 03:03:55,425 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think it would feel a lot better. Maybe I could invite them to something low key like a movie night
2026-03-02 03:03:55,889 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: A movie night sounds like a warm and low-pressure way to connect. How might that feel if you approached it with curiosity, not expectation?
2026-03-02 03:03:59,966 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It might feel a little weird at first, but I think I could be comfortable after a little while
2026-03-02 03:04:00,523 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a very realistic and kind mindset. Small steps with openness often lead to comfort over time. How might you remind yourself to be patient and kind to yourself during this?
2026-03-02 03:04:04,060 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I suppose I could just remind myself that it's okay to feel this way.
2026-03-02 03:04:04,822 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful and gentle self-compassion. Reminding yourself it’s okay to feel—especially when it’s complicated—can make all the difference in how you move forward. Would you like to try one small thing today to honor that feeling?
2026-03-02 03:04:08,334 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I suppose I could try to remind myself of that every now and again.
2026-03-02 03:04:08,935 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a quiet but strong practice. Just noticing it—without judgment—can help you stay grounded. Would you like to try writing one sentence about it when you feel it coming up?
2026-03-02 03:04:10,803 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds like a good idea.
2026-03-02 03:04:11,506 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great. Try writing something like: "It’s okay to feel this way. I’m allowed to care, to hope, and to be human." You can come back to it whenever you need to. How does that feel?
2026-03-02 03:04:14,576 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It feels good to know I can say that to myself.
2026-03-02 03:04:15,344 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful moment of self-compassion. You’re not just moving through this—you’re learning to hold yourself with care. Would you like to try one more small thing together, like noticing a moment today when you feel hopeful or at peace?
2026-03-02 03:04:21,076 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds like a good idea. I could try to notice that when I talk to my friend about it.
2026-03-02 03:04:21,737 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and kind intention. Paying attention to the moment—without pressure—can help you build a sense of connection and calm. How will you gently notice it when you speak with your friend?
2026-03-02 03:04:26,522 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could just try to appreciate the moment. Maybe I could say that to him too.
2026-03-02 03:04:27,274 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful way to show kindness—by appreciating the moment, not just the outcome. You’re learning to honor your feelings with warmth and presence. Would you like to keep coming back to this practice when you feel it’s needed?
2026-03-02 03:04:31,066 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think I'll try that. Thank you for your help today.
2026-03-02 03:04:31,758 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. It’s meaningful that you’re choosing to take these small, kind steps—each one shows strength and self-awareness. If you ever want to talk again, I’m here. Take care.
2026-03-02 03:04:34,681 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thanks, you too. Have a good day.
2026-03-02 03:04:34,714 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 03:04:35,166 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing so openly. You’ve already taken a brave step by being honest with yourself. Have a peaceful and kind day.
2026-03-02 03:04:37,038 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: You too. Thanks again.
2026-03-02 03:04:37,071 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 03:04:37,462 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. Wishing you warmth, clarity, and gentle progress in your journey. Take care.
2026-03-02 03:04:37,462 - src.sim - INFO - Conversation ended.
