2026-03-02 03:04:39,693 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 03:04:44,123 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I'm feeling very sad and jealous today. My cousin is doing very well for herself and I feel that I helped her when she needed it and now I'm not appreciated.
2026-03-02 03:04:44,631 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like you're carrying a sense of unmet appreciation despite your efforts. What specific moment or event has made that feeling of jealousy and sadness stronger?
2026-03-02 03:04:51,270 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel this way a lot but I saw my cousin's post on Facebook saying she bought herself a new handbag for her birthday. It made me feel like I'm destined to be in poverty and can't even afford to dream.
2026-03-02 03:04:51,788 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That post must have triggered a strong comparison. What do you think your cousin’s message about the handbag might represent in your mind—something about success, value, or being overlooked?
2026-03-02 03:04:55,235 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes it does. It makes me feel like she's very successful and happy and I'm not appreciated at all.
2026-03-02 03:04:55,946 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It sounds like the handbag symbolizes success and recognition for her, which makes you feel unseen or undervalued. What if that bag isn’t a measure of worth—but a reflection of her personal joy, which doesn’t have to compare to your own?
2026-03-02 03:05:00,903 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes that's true. I'm sure she's happy and that's what matters most. I just feel jealous because I can't buy things like that right now.
2026-03-02 03:05:01,608 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s natural to feel jealous when you compare your current situation to someone else’s achievements. What if that jealousy isn’t about buying things, but about your own worth being questioned? What would a small, meaningful action today tell you about your own value?
2026-03-02 03:05:06,158 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I suppose it's about questioning my worth. I do have moments where I feel I am worth something and it does make me feel better.
2026-03-02 03:05:06,746 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful insight—your moments of feeling worthy are real and valid. What might those moments look like? For example, when do you feel most proud of yourself, even if it’s small?
2026-03-02 03:05:12,744 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel most proud of myself when I'm helping others, especially family members who are in need. I feel like I'm being the best version of myself when I'm helping others.
2026-03-02 03:05:13,359 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful and important realization. Your sense of value comes from being present and supportive—something deeply meaningful. What if that kind of strength and care is already a form of success, even without material things?
2026-03-02 03:05:19,145 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I believe it is a form of success and I should feel proud of that. I just need to be more positive and focus on myself and what I can achieve.
2026-03-02 03:05:19,750 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're already on a strong path by recognizing your value in caring for others. What would a small, daily act of self-affirmation—like writing one thing you did well—look like for you?
2026-03-02 03:05:24,077 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I could say to myself "I'm a caring person and I am capable of achieving my goals" after each day.
2026-03-02 03:05:24,675 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful and simple affirmation. Starting with just one sentence each day can help build a stronger sense of self-worth over time. How would you feel if you tried that for just one week?
2026-03-02 03:05:27,803 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel I would feel a lot better about myself and I would be more positive.
2026-03-02 03:05:28,503 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a hopeful and realistic outcome. You’re not just changing your thoughts—you’re building a new way of seeing yourself. Would you like to try saying that affirmation out loud in a quiet moment today, just to feel it in your body?
2026-03-02 03:05:31,148 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes I feel I will feel better if I say it out loud.
2026-03-02 03:05:31,816 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great. You’re taking a meaningful step by choosing to speak those words into your day. Let me know how it feels when you say it—especially when you notice your body or breath respond. That’s part of the process.
2026-03-02 03:05:35,893 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you for your help today. I will say my affirmation now and let you know how I feel afterwards.
2026-03-02 03:05:36,485 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. I'm glad we could walk this together. Take your time with the affirmation—when you're ready, just let me know how it felt. I'm here for you.
2026-03-02 03:05:42,369 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I said my affirmation and I do feel a lot better. I'm more positive and I feel like I'm a lot more confident in myself now.
2026-03-02 03:05:42,402 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 03:05:43,127 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s wonderful to hear. Your confidence is growing, not because you’ve changed overnight, but because you’ve been consistent in choosing self-worth. Keep returning to that affirmation when it helps—you're building a stronger sense of yourself, one moment at a time.
2026-03-02 03:05:45,820 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you for your help today. I feel much better now.
2026-03-02 03:05:45,857 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 03:05:46,541 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You're very welcome. I'm glad your affirmation brought you peace and confidence. Remember, you don't have to do it perfectly—just keep coming back to it when you need to. I'm here whenever you'd like to talk again.
