2026-03-02 03:09:41,006 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 03:09:45,019 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Hello, I am feeling pretty bad. I have a lot of suicidal thoughts and I am having a lot of self harm urges today.
2026-03-02 03:09:45,698 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that with me. I'm really sorry you're going through this—it sounds incredibly difficult. Can you tell me more about what's happening right now? For example, when did these thoughts first start, and what might be triggering them?
2026-03-02 03:09:50,495 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well, I'm currently at college and I'm almost done. I'm graduating in May and I'm worried about moving back in with my parents after graduation.
2026-03-02 03:09:51,308 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. It sounds like the transition to living with your parents after graduation is causing a lot of anxiety. That kind of change can be overwhelming, especially when you're already feeling emotionally strained. How does that worry about returning home affect your current feelings of sadness or self-harm urges?
2026-03-02 03:09:57,386 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I have been doing pretty well in college because I have a close friend group that I hang out with a lot. They are very supportive and help me feel better when I am feeling down.
2026-03-02 03:09:58,211 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s really important—having a supportive friend group can make a big difference in tough times. It sounds like you’re already building resilience through those connections. How does being with your friends specifically help you feel better when you're struggling? Is there a particular thing they do or say that makes a difference?
2026-03-02 03:10:03,046 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I am very thankful for my friends. They distract me from my negative thoughts and they remind me that I am loved and that I am important.
2026-03-02 03:10:03,769 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s deeply meaningful. It sounds like your friends are not just offering distraction, but genuine emotional support that helps you reconnect with your worth. Can you think of a specific moment when their words or actions really helped you feel seen or safe during a difficult time?
2026-03-02 03:10:08,710 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: My friend, Abraham, has been very helpful to me. He has been very encouraging and reminds me that he is always there for me.
2026-03-02 03:10:09,560 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for sharing that. Abraham’s consistency and presence seem to be a strong anchor for you. It’s powerful that he makes space to remind you he’s there—something that can feel like a lifeline in hard moments. Is there a particular time or conversation with Abraham that stands out as especially comforting?
2026-03-02 03:10:13,530 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel that he has been there for me in so many times that I don't even remember them all.
2026-03-02 03:10:14,522 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful thing—feeling supported without needing to remember every moment can still carry deep emotional weight. The fact that he’s been steady and present, even when you didn’t notice, shows how quietly and deeply love can show up. Would you like to think about how you might gently express your gratitude to Abraham in a way that feels meaningful to you?
2026-03-02 03:10:20,690 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you, I will definitely do that! I also feel that I need to repress my feelings and needs in order to not burden my loved ones with my depression.
2026-03-02 03:10:21,560 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a really important insight—feeling the need to suppress your emotions to protect others can be exhausting and deeply painful. It shows how much you care about your relationships. What might happen if you allowed yourself to share your feelings with someone, even just a little, without expecting them to fix or change anything?
2026-03-02 03:10:28,911 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel that if I allow myself to share my feelings I may feel better and it may help others understand me better, but I still feel like I may be burdening them with my sadness
2026-03-02 03:10:30,097 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely valid to feel that way—your fear of burdening others is rooted in love and care. But sharing your feelings doesn’t mean you’re asking them to carry your pain; it means you’re giving yourself permission to be seen. What if you started with a small, gentle gesture—like telling one person, “I’ve been feeling really heavy lately, and I wanted to share it with you”? How would that feel?
2026-03-02 03:10:35,300 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds like it could be very helpful and comforting, but I still feel that it is hard for me to express my sadness.
2026-03-02 03:10:36,283 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely normal to feel hesitant—especially when sadness has been something you’ve carried quietly for a long time. The fact that you're even considering sharing is a brave step. What might be one small way you could begin to express yourself, without pressure or expectation? Maybe just a note, a text, or a quiet moment with someone you trust?
2026-03-02 03:10:41,239 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think that I may be able to share my feelings with someone if they are there for me and offer emotional support.
2026-03-02 03:10:42,171 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a very important and hopeful insight. You’re not looking for validation or to fix things—you’re seeking connection with someone who will meet you with care and presence. What if you started by imagining a conversation where you share just one feeling, and they respond with kindness, not judgment? How does that image make you feel?
2026-03-02 03:10:48,641 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That sounds very comforting and it gives me hope for the future! I am just worried about how I will cope without my college friends once I graduate.
2026-03-02 03:10:49,694 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely natural to feel that worry—your friends have been a crucial part of your emotional support system. What if you began thinking of your future not as a loss, but as an opportunity to build new connections that also offer care and safety? Is there one person or activity you’d like to try reaching out to, even in small ways, once you’re home?
2026-03-02 03:10:54,832 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I would like to reach out to my pastor and maybe take some art classes at the local community center.
2026-03-02 03:10:55,775 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and hopeful direction. Reaching out to your pastor could offer a space of trust and care, and art classes might provide a gentle, creative way to express what you're feeling. What might it feel like to start with a simple message or a quiet visit to the community center—just to explore the idea?
2026-03-02 03:11:01,755 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I feel that if I take these steps, I may feel better and it will give me hope for the future!
2026-03-02 03:11:02,731 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s truly powerful—your courage in taking these small, intentional steps is already changing things. Each small action you choose builds a foundation of hope, even when it feels fragile. Would you like to think about how you might begin with one of these steps this week, like sending a short message to your pastor or visiting the community center?
2026-03-02 03:11:09,534 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, I will try that. I will also try to stop cutting myself because I know that it does not help my situation.
2026-03-02 03:11:10,592 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a very important and courageous decision. Stopping self-harm is not about strength alone—it’s about choosing care for yourself, even when it feels hard. You’re already taking a vital step by recognizing that it doesn’t serve you. How might you gently honor that choice in your daily life—perhaps by noticing when the urge comes and offering yourself kindness instead?
2026-03-02 03:11:16,636 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I will try to distract myself from the urges and remind myself that I am loved and that I am strong.
2026-03-02 03:11:17,634 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful and effective strategy. Distracting yourself with awareness—rather than reacting to the urge—gives you space to reconnect with your inner strength. And reminding yourself you’re loved and strong is a powerful act of self-compassion. Would you like to try naming one small thing you’ll do today to honor that truth?
2026-03-02 03:11:27,496 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, I will do my best to do that. I also feel that I should reach out to my friends before they leave college for the summer and thank them for their support.
2026-03-02 03:11:28,486 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful and kind gesture—reaching out to thank your friends shows how much their support has mattered to you. It also strengthens your connection to them, even as life changes. What if you start by writing a short message to one of them, just to say how much their presence has helped you? How does that feel?
2026-03-02 03:11:33,077 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That feels great! I will do that soon. Thank you for your help!
2026-03-02 03:11:34,098 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You’re very welcome. Thank you for being so honest and brave in sharing your journey. Those small, kind actions you’re choosing—reaching out, honoring your strength, and saying thank you—are already healing. If you ever want to talk more or just sit with something that’s on your mind, I’m here. Take care of yourself.
2026-03-02 03:11:39,029 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you so much. I will definitely try your suggestions. Have a great day!
2026-03-02 03:11:39,070 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 03:11:39,742 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You’ve got this. Every step you take, no matter how small, is a sign of strength. I’m truly grateful you shared this with me. Wishing you peace, hope, and comfort today. Take care.
2026-03-02 03:11:43,390 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you. I hope you have a great day too.
2026-03-02 03:11:43,428 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 03:11:44,017 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for your kind words. I’m so glad I could support you. Wishing you warmth, calm, and a sense of peace as you move forward. Have a wonderful day.
2026-03-02 03:11:44,017 - src.sim - INFO - Conversation ended.
