2026-03-02 22:40:23,013 - src.sim - INFO - Expanded patient history: Over the past week, I’ve been going through a lot of quiet emotional turmoil. I’ve been trying to keep things light with my friends, especially with the girl I’ve been seeing as a friend. At first, I thought it was just a friendship—something casual and safe. But as time went on, I started to notice how much I wanted to be closer to her, even in small ways: the way she smiled when she saw me, how she’d ask about my day, how she’d listen without judgment. It felt like a slow kind of pull, something I couldn’t ignore. I kept telling myself it was just friendship, but deep down, I knew it wasn’t. I felt lonely, even when I was around people I cared about.

When I finally found out she had feelings for my close friend, I was really hurt. I didn’t expect it to happen that way. I didn’t want to cause any conflict, so I just tried to stay calm. I told her my friend was a great match for her, and I even said something like, “I’m really glad you’re happy with someone who makes you feel safe.” I said it with a smile, but inside, I felt like I was burying myself under layers of sadness. I didn’t want to make her feel uncomfortable or hurt, so I chose silence over honesty. I kept thinking, *Maybe if she knew how much I cared, she’d see things differently.* But I didn’t say it. I just let it sit in me, like a weight I couldn’t lift.

There was one moment when I was sitting by myself after a group hangout, and I realized something—she had said early on that she wanted things to stay just friends. I remember her saying, “We’re not going anywhere serious, I just like hanging out with you.” And I just brushed it off. I thought, *It’s just a friendship*, and I didn’t take it seriously. Now I realize how much I let that pass. I didn’t see it as a boundary, and I didn’t understand how much my feelings were growing in the shadows. That made me feel ashamed—like I’d been blind, and like I’d let myself get caught in something that wasn’t fair to anyone.

I’ve been trying to understand why I didn’t speak up or reach out. I know I’ve been quiet, avoiding the pain, just keeping my emotions locked inside. But now, with you pointing that out, I can see it more clearly. I didn’t want to ruin anything, and I thought if I stayed silent, everything would stay peaceful. But peace isn’t built on silence. I think I’ve been trying to protect others—my friend, her, even myself—by pretending everything is fine. And now I realize that my worth isn’t measured by whether someone chooses to love me or not. It’s not about being chosen. It’s about being honest with myself and with others. That’s the first step I need to take.
