2026-03-02 23:02:17,957 - src.sim - INFO - Expanded patient history: Over the past week, I’ve been going through a lot of quiet pain—something I’ve tried to keep hidden from everyone, especially my friends. I started spending more time thinking about her, not just as a friend, but as someone who could possibly mean something more. At first, I thought maybe she was just being kind or friendly, but as time went on, I found myself feeling more and more drawn to her. I remember when she first said she liked spending time with me, I didn’t think much of it. I figured that was just how friendships work—people get close, share things, and that’s it. But then I began to notice small things—how she’d smile when I talked about my hobbies, how she’d ask about my day even when I didn’t say much. It started to feel like she was seeing me in a way I wasn’t expecting.  

When I found out she had feelings for my best friend, I felt a sharp pain in my chest. I didn’t want to be the one who caused discomfort, so I just kept quiet. I told myself that if she was happy, that was better than me being sad. I even tried to say nice things to her, like how my friend was a great match for her—calm, grounded, someone who really understood her. I did that mostly to protect her, to not make her feel bad or like she had to choose between us. But deep down, I knew I was hurting myself. I kept trying to stay cheerful around everyone, even when I felt like I was crumbling inside. I didn’t want to bring up how much I cared, because I thought it would make things worse—especially with my friend, who I still care about deeply.  

I’ve been thinking a lot about what I should have done earlier. I remember when she first mentioned she wanted to keep things as friends, and I brushed it off. I didn’t realize how much I was already investing in that friendship. Now I see that maybe I didn’t give her space, or respect her boundaries, because I was too focused on what I wanted. I feel embarrassed to admit it, but I think I let my own emotions cloud my judgment. I wasn’t just trying to be a good friend—I was trying to be loved, and I didn’t realize that love isn’t something you have to earn through someone else’s attention.  

Now, when I think about it, I realize that I didn’t just want her to like me—I wanted her to *see* me. And that’s not the same as being loved. I think I made a mistake by letting my feelings grow so deep without asking for clarity. I don’t know if I’ll ever be able to move past this, but I do know that I need to stop hiding my pain. I need to be honest with myself—about how I feel, and about how I’ve been treating her and myself. I don’t want to be someone who sacrifices his own happiness for others’ peace. I want to learn how to be okay with being seen, even if it’s not in a way I imagined.
