2026-03-02 23:05:16,106 - src.sim - INFO - Expanded patient history: Over the past week, I’ve been going through a lot of quiet pain—something I’ve tried to keep hidden from everyone, especially my friends. I started seeing this girl a few months ago, and at first, we just hung out. We were both honest about wanting to stay friends, and I thought that was enough. I didn’t push anything, and I respected that. But over time, something changed. I found myself thinking about her more—more than I should have. I’d catch myself staring at her when she walked by, or remembering small things she said, and I’d start wondering if she ever saw me the way I saw her.  

It wasn’t until I found out she had been spending time with my close friend that the pain really set in. I didn’t want to confront her or even talk about it, because I feared it would make things awkward. I didn’t want to make her uncomfortable, or worse, to make my friend feel guilty. So instead, I just kept quiet. I told myself she deserved to be happy, that she should be with someone who made her feel safe and supported—someone like my friend. I even tried to say that to her, in a gentle way, like I was just being thoughtful. But inside, I felt like I was failing myself. I felt like I wasn’t worthy of anything real, not even a simple friendship that included feeling something deeper.  

I’ve been trying to stay upbeat around everyone, especially my friend. I make jokes, I smile, I even go out with them when I used to just want to be alone. But I can’t shake the feeling that I’ve been ignoring something important—like her initial message saying they were just friends. I should have noticed that earlier. Maybe I didn’t see it clearly because I was too focused on my own feelings. Now, when I think about it, I realize I didn’t ask her how she really felt, or what she wanted. I just assumed that if I stayed quiet and didn’t push, everything would be fine. That’s not how love—or even friendship—works.  

Now, hearing that I might have missed the boundaries in the beginning makes me feel ashamed. Not just because I got hurt, but because I let myself believe that if I didn’t express my feelings, it would somehow protect others. But in reality, I’ve been holding back not because I’m strong, but because I thought I wasn’t good enough. I’m starting to understand that my emotions aren’t a burden—they’re a part of who I am. And maybe, just maybe, I don’t have to hide them anymore.
