2026-03-02 23:07:40,415 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor greeting: Hello, how are you feeling today? Any concerns or feelings you'd like to share? I'm here to listen and help with whatever you're going through.
2026-03-02 23:07:42,473 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I’ve been feeling so lost, like I don’t know what to do.
2026-03-02 23:07:42,958 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That sounds really difficult. Can you tell me more about what’s been making that sense of being lost feel so strong lately? Maybe a specific situation or thought that comes up?
2026-03-02 23:07:47,081 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well, I found out my boyfriend cheated on me with my best friend. Now he’s planning to propose to me, but with her as a bridesmaid.
2026-03-02 23:07:47,673 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That must feel incredibly painful and confusing. What emotions are most present right now—anger, sadness, betrayal, or something else? It sounds like your feelings are being tested in a way that’s hard to process.
2026-03-02 23:07:50,015 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I guess sadness and confusion. It feels like I don’t know what’s true anymore.
2026-03-02 23:07:50,741 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s completely valid to feel uncertain when trust is broken like that. What if we explored one thought you might have—like “I don’t know what’s true anymore”—and looked at evidence that supports it and evidence that challenges it? Would that be helpful?
2026-03-02 23:07:52,594 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, I think that could help me understand things better.
2026-03-02 23:07:53,247 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great. Let’s start with this thought: “I don’t know what’s true anymore.” What specific evidence do you have that supports this feeling? For example, what moments or experiences have made you feel uncertain about what’s real?
2026-03-02 23:07:58,838 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: When I first found out about the affair, I didn’t believe it because my friend is so close to me. It took me a long time to realize that I was being lied to.
2026-03-02 23:07:59,710 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That makes sense—your trust in that relationship was deeply rooted, so the betrayal must have felt like a collapse of something familiar. What part of the situation do you think is hardest to accept now—your friend’s role, your boyfriend’s actions, or the idea that you can’t fully trust what you once believed?
2026-03-02 23:08:03,935 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Probably the idea that I can’t fully trust what I once believed. I always trusted my friends and my boyfriend to make the right decisions.
2026-03-02 23:08:04,788 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s natural to have trusted those people so deeply—trust is built over time, and it’s not something you can just erase. What might be a small step you could take to begin rebuilding a sense of clarity, like checking in with a trusted person or writing down what you’ve learned about this situation?
2026-03-02 23:08:07,924 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe talking to my family about it, or writing down everything I know and feeling about it.
2026-03-02 23:08:08,631 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Both of those steps could help bring clarity and peace. What would feel most manageable to start with—talking to someone you trust, or writing it out in detail? Maybe we can explore which one feels more like a natural next step for you.
2026-03-02 23:08:10,683 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think writing it out in detail would be more manageable.
2026-03-02 23:08:11,405 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a thoughtful and courageous choice. Writing can help you see your feelings and experiences more clearly without the pressure of immediate conversation. Would you like to start by writing about the first moment you realized something was wrong—or the moment you felt the truth shift?
2026-03-02 23:08:14,635 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think the first moment I realized something was wrong would be a good place to start.
2026-03-02 23:08:15,275 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great. What did you notice in that moment—was it a specific word, tone, behavior, or a shift in something you’d always trusted? Even small details can help clarify what you felt and why it mattered.
2026-03-02 23:08:20,105 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Well, it was more of a tone, like when my friend told me that my boyfriend was at her house for a sleepover.
2026-03-02 23:08:20,871 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: A tone can carry so much weight—especially when it’s unexpected. What did that tone feel like to you? Was it casual, warm, or something else that made you pause? How did it contrast with how you usually experienced your relationship or your friend’s behavior?
2026-03-02 23:08:24,701 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: The tone was casual, but it felt different because I couldn’t sense any warmth or affection from her.
2026-03-02 23:08:25,412 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful detail. A casual tone without warmth can feel cold or disconnected—especially when it comes from someone you trusted. How did that lack of warmth make you feel in that moment? Did it trigger a memory or emotion from before?
2026-03-02 23:08:30,186 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It made me feel uncomfortable, like something wasn’t right. But I couldn’t quite put my finger on it at the time.
2026-03-02 23:08:30,968 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: It’s interesting that you felt discomfort without knowing exactly why—sometimes, our gut feelings are the first signs of something shifting. What might have happened if you’d paid more attention to that discomfort at the time? Could it have changed how you responded to the situation later?
2026-03-02 23:08:34,173 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe I would have caught on sooner, or been able to prevent the affair.
2026-03-02 23:08:35,198 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a meaningful reflection. It shows how your intuition was already working, even if you didn’t recognize it. What if we gently consider this: instead of seeing that discomfort as a failure, we see it as a signal that something important was out of alignment—like a quiet alarm in your heart? Would that shift help you feel a bit more in control?
2026-03-02 23:08:41,118 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, that helps. It’s less like a failure, and more like I didn’t have the tools to deal with the situation at the time.
2026-03-02 23:08:41,998 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a very honest and empowering realization. You didn’t fail—you simply didn’t have the tools to process that kind of pain back then. Now, with awareness, you’re building your own tools to understand and protect your emotional well-being. What would you like to do next to support that growth?
2026-03-02 23:08:45,486 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think writing out the rest of the story would be a good place to start.
2026-03-02 23:08:46,383 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a strong and thoughtful next step. Writing the rest of the story can help you see the full picture and your place in it—without judgment. Would you like to start by writing about what happened after that moment of discomfort, or what you noticed in your relationship with your boyfriend and friend since then?
2026-03-02 23:08:49,504 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think writing about what happened after would be the next best step.
2026-03-02 23:08:50,281 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Great choice. Writing about what followed can help you see patterns and your emotional journey more clearly. What might be the first detail you’d like to include—like a specific conversation, a change in behavior, or a moment when you felt especially hurt or confused?
2026-03-02 23:08:54,044 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe the moment when I found out about the affair, and how I felt after.
2026-03-02 23:08:54,928 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a powerful starting point. What did your body or mind feel like in that moment—shaken, numb, angry, or something else? Try to describe it as if you’re telling it to someone who’s never heard it before. Would that help you feel more grounded in what happened?
2026-03-02 23:08:59,578 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I felt so betrayed, like my trust had been broken in a way that seemed irreparable.
2026-03-02 23:09:00,439 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a very real and deeply felt reaction. To have your trust—something you built over years—shattered in that way is not just painful; it changes how you see relationships forever. How might that feeling of irreparable loss still show up in your daily life, even now?
2026-03-02 23:09:06,153 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think it shows up when I’m making decisions about my relationship or when I feel betrayed by other people.
2026-03-02 23:09:07,170 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That makes sense—when trust is deeply damaged, it can become a kind of emotional lens. You might not be “reacting” to betrayal, but rather recognizing patterns from that moment. What if, instead of seeing that as a sign of weakness, you see it as a sign that you’re still learning how to protect yourself in meaningful connections?
2026-03-02 23:09:10,938 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: That makes sense, and helps me feel more positive about the situation.
2026-03-02 23:09:11,784 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: I’m glad it’s helping. You’re not just processing pain—you’re building awareness and strength. Would you like to continue writing about what happened after that moment, or would you prefer to take a moment to reflect on how you’d like to move forward in your relationships now?
2026-03-02 23:09:17,581 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think taking a moment to reflect on how I’d like to move forward would be a good next step.
2026-03-02 23:09:18,317 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a wise and courageous choice. What does “moving forward” feel like to you—more peaceful, more intentional, or simply more honest with yourself? Is there one small step you could take to honor that vision without pressure?
2026-03-02 23:09:27,903 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: It would feel like moving forward to be more intentional, to be honest with myself. A small step I could take is to follow my heart and see where it leads me.
2026-03-02 23:09:27,941 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 23:09:28,848 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a beautiful and grounded direction. Following your heart doesn’t mean ignoring your pain—it means honoring your values and your need for trust, safety, and truth in relationships. What might that look like in the next week—like a quiet moment to check in with yourself, or a conversation with someone you trust?
2026-03-02 23:09:34,764 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Maybe just taking time to journal about my feelings, or to talk with someone who is supportive and understanding.
2026-03-02 23:09:35,663 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Those are gentle, meaningful steps. Journaling gives you space to stay connected to your inner world, and talking with someone supportive helps you feel seen without judgment. How would you like to start—writing one short entry each day, or choosing one supportive person to share with in the next week?
2026-03-02 23:09:40,533 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: I think starting with one short entry each day would be a good place to start.
2026-03-02 23:09:41,332 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: That’s a sustainable and powerful habit. A single short entry—just a few sentences about what you’re feeling, noticing, or wondering—can help you stay grounded and kind to yourself. Would you like to try writing one together as a starting point?
2026-03-02 23:09:46,269 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Yes, I think that would be a great idea. Thank you for your help.
2026-03-02 23:09:47,249 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You’re very welcome. It’s clear you’re on a thoughtful and brave path. Remember, this journey isn’t about fixing anything—it’s about understanding yourself more deeply and honoring your feelings. If you’d like to return or explore anything further, I’m here. Take care, and thank you for sharing this with me.
2026-03-02 23:09:50,122 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: Thank you, and have a great day.
2026-03-02 23:09:50,160 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 1 times.
2026-03-02 23:09:50,802 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: You’re very welcome. I’m glad I could support you. Take care of yourself, and remember—you’re not alone in this. If anything comes up, I’m here. Have a peaceful day.
2026-03-02 23:09:52,821 - src.sim - INFO - Patient: You too. Thank you.
2026-03-02 23:09:52,854 - src.doctor - INFO - Doctor has detected end of conversation 2 times.
2026-03-02 23:09:53,476 - src.sim - INFO - Doctor: Thank you for your honesty and courage. You’ve already taken a powerful step by being willing to reflect. I’ll be here when you’re ready. Take care, and have a gentle day.
2026-03-02 23:09:53,476 - src.sim - INFO - Conversation ended.
